#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:6
msgid "GPU interrupt handling: GIN and the GSP event"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:8
msgid ""
"This document describes how nova-core receives interrupts from the GPU on "
"Turing and later chipsets. It covers the GPU Interrupt and Notification unit "
"(GIN), which is the GPU's interrupt controller, and the GSP event, the "
"interrupt that nova-core services in normal operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:13
msgid ""
"Throughout, *CPU* means the CPU and the nova-core driver running on it. The "
"GPU also has on-chip processors that run their own firmware and receive "
"their own interrupts. The GSP (GPU System Processor) is one of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:17
msgid ""
"Register names are the names from the GPU hardware reference headers. The "
"pre-Hopper headers call the controller ``NV_CTRL`` and the Hopper-plus "
"headers call it ``NV_GIN``. This document calls it GIN throughout, because "
"the tree that nova-core services is the same on every supported chipset. "
"OpenRM, NVIDIA's open-source GPU kernel driver, is cited wherever nova-core "
"follows it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:24
msgid "Terminology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:26
msgid "The three levels of the controller, innermost first:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:28
msgid "leaf"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:29
msgid ""
"One ``LEAF`` register. Each of its 32 bits is the pending bit of one "
"interrupt source. A Turing, Ampere, or Ada tree has 8 leaves. A Hopper or "
"Blackwell tree has 16."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:33
msgid "subtree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:34
msgid ""
"Two consecutive leaves, summarized by one bit of ``TOP``. A subtree is the "
"unit of enabling at ``TOP``, and under MSI-X it is the unit of delivery: "
"every interrupt from one subtree arrives on one MSI-X entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:38
msgid "tree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:39
msgid ""
"One ``TOP`` register and the leaves under it. Every PCIe function has its "
"own tree, and nova-core services the CPU tree of one function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:42
msgid ""
"The hardware headers, OpenRM, and the Linux PCI API all use the word "
"\"vector\", each for a different number. This document gives each one its "
"own name, and a bare \"vector\" always means a GIN vector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:46
msgid "GIN vector"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:47
msgid ""
"The GPU-internal interrupt source number. It addresses one bit of one leaf. "
"A 16-leaf tree holds vectors 0 through 511, and an 8-leaf tree holds 0 "
"through 255. The CPU doorbell is vector 129 and the GSP event is vector 155."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:52
msgid "MSI-X entry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:53
msgid "An index into the device's MSI-X table. One entry serves one subtree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:55
msgid "PCI vector"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:56
msgid ""
"One of the interrupts that ``pci_alloc_irq_vectors()`` allocates: an MSI-X "
"entry, or the single MSI message."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:59
msgid "Linux IRQ number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:60
msgid ""
"What ``request_irq()`` takes, obtained from ``pci_irq_vector()`` for a PCI "
"vector. Linux's ``struct msix_entry`` calls this number ``.vector`` as well."
msgstr ""

