msgid "rearm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:77
msgid ""
"Restoring PCI interrupt delivery after servicing an interrupt. See "
"\"Rearming PCI interrupt delivery\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:80
msgid "mask"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:81
msgid ""
"Reserved for the two places where hardware and the PCI specification use the "
"word: the MSI-X per-entry Vector Control mask bit, which Linux controls, and "
"the falcon interrupt masks. It never names a GIN enable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:85
msgid "latched, pending"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:86
msgid ""
"Two names for one state, a set ``LEAF`` bit. The vector's source sets the "
"bit whether or not the vector is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:89
msgid "clear a vector"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:90
msgid ""
"Write a 1 to the vector's bit in ``LEAF``. OpenRM calls the same operation "
"``intrClearLeafVector_HAL``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:93
msgid "pending bits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:94
msgid "The plain 32-bit value read from a ``LEAF`` register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:96
msgid "notification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:97
msgid ""
"An interrupt whose only content is that something happened, such as a posted "
"message. Servicing a notification means reading what it announces. The unit "
"that raised it needs no attention. The GSP event is one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:101
msgid "unit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:102
msgid ""
"Any block that raises an interrupt. \"Engine\" is reserved for the blocks "
"that do user work: GR, CE, NVDEC, and the like."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:105
msgid "falcon"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:106
msgid ""
"One of the GPU's microcontrollers (see Documentation/gpu/nova/core/"
"falcon.rst). The GSP runs on the RISC-V core inside its falcon. A falcon "
"latches each of its interrupt causes and routes it either to the host, "
"meaning the CPU, or to its own core."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:112
msgid "The GIN controller"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:114
msgid ""
"A GPU has many interrupt sources: the GSP, the copy engines, the graphics "
"engine, video decode and encode, the MMU fault path, timers, and others. GIN "
"records which of them are pending and raises the PCI interrupt to the CPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:119
msgid "Trees"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:121
msgid ""
"GIN keeps one tree for each destination it can deliver an interrupt to. The "
"CPU has one tree per PCIe function, so the physical function and each "
"virtual function have their own. The GSP has a tree, and so do the other on-"
"chip processors that receive interrupts. Every tree has the same two-level "
"layout, and a function reaches its own tree through the per-function "
"register aperture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:127
msgid ""
"nova-core services the CPU tree of one function. A virtual function's tree "
"belongs to that function's driver, and a processor's tree belongs to the "
"firmware running on that processor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:132
msgid "The two-level tree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:134
msgid "A tree is a set of ``LEAF`` registers and one ``TOP`` register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:136
msgid ""
"``LEAF(i)`` is a 32-bit register that holds the pending bits of vectors "
"``32i`` through ``32i + 31``. A set bit is a pending vector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:138
msgid ""
"``TOP`` is a 32-bit read-only register. Bit ``N`` summarizes subtree ``N``, "
"which is ``LEAF(2N)`` and ``LEAF(2N + 1)``. The bit is set when an enabled "
"vector is pending in either leaf."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:142
msgid ""
"A tree with L leaves has L / 2 subtrees and uses TOP bits 0 through L / 2 - "
"1. The other TOP bits read 0. The leaves and subtrees that a chipset has are "
"its implemented leaves and subtrees, and \"Per-architecture differences\" "
"gives the counts. For an 8-leaf tree::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:157
msgid "A vector's number fixes its place in the tree::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:164
msgid "Registers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:166
msgid ""
"nova-core defines the tree's registers in the ``irq`` module's ``regs.rs``. "
"The leaf registers are arrays indexed by leaf number."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:169
msgid ""
"``LEAF(i)`` reads as the pending bits of leaf ``i``. Writing a 1 to a bit "
"clears that vector, and a 0 leaves the bit as it was."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:171
msgid ""
"``LEAF_EN_SET(i)`` and ``LEAF_EN_CLEAR(i)`` enable and disable the vectors "
"of leaf ``i``, one bit per vector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:173
msgid ""
"``TOP_EN_SET`` and ``TOP_EN_CLEAR`` enable and disable subtrees, one bit per "
"subtree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:175
msgid ""
"``LEAF_TRIGGER`` takes a vector number and latches that vector, exactly as "
"the vector's own source would. It is write-only. The self-test uses it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:178
msgid "nova-core does not read ``TOP``. \"Servicing the tree\" says why."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:180
msgid ""
"Every set and clear register acts per bit: a 1 performs the action for that "
"bit, and a 0 leaves the bit alone. No register needs a read-modify-write."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:183
msgid ""
"GIN delivers a vector to the CPU only when its leaf enable bit and its "
"subtree's TOP enable bit are both set. The enables do not affect the latch. "
"The source of a disabled vector still sets its ``LEAF`` bit. ``TOP`` does "
"not show that bit, so reading the leaf is the only way to see it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:189
msgid "How a unit interrupt reaches the CPU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:191
msgid ""
"A unit does not write a ``LEAF`` register. Each unit has an interrupt "
"control register that GSP firmware programs. The control register holds the "
"unit's vector, the GFID that identifies the PCIe function whose tree "
"receives the interrupt, and one enable bit per destination: the CPU, the "
"GSP, and the other on-chip processors. When the unit has an event::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:204
msgid ""
"Because firmware assigns the vectors, nova-core does not hardcode which "
"vector belongs to which unit, with two exceptions. The hardware headers of "
"every supported chipset define the GSP event as vector 155, and GSP "
"firmware's own interrupt table uses that definition. The CPU doorbell is "
"vector 129. Pre-Hopper hardware fixes that number, and GSP firmware keeps it "
"on Hopper and later. nova-core names both by number. A driver can fetch the "
"full unit-to-vector table from the GSP by RPC, and nouveau does. nova-core "
"does not, because a fixed vector needs no lookup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:214
msgid "Edge-triggered delivery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:216
msgid ""
"A ``LEAF`` bit is a latch. Its source sets it on a rising edge, and it stays "
"set until the CPU clears it. A source that stays high does not set the bit "
"again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:219
msgid ""
"GIN raises the PCI interrupt for a subtree when the subtree's enabled "
"pending state goes from low to high::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:231
msgid ""
"``TOP_EN`` applies after the summary, so disabling a subtree stops delivery "
"without changing what ``TOP`` reports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:234
msgid "Three consequences:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:236
msgid ""
"Code that must find every pending vector reads the leaves. A vector that "
"latched while disabled is not in ``TOP``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:238
msgid ""
"Writing ``TOP_EN_SET`` for a subtree with an enabled pending bit produces a "
"new edge. GIN delivers an interrupt for a pending bit left uncleared as soon "
"as its subtree is enabled again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:241
msgid ""
"A source that holds its signal high produces no new edge after the CPU "
"clears the leaf bit. Such a source has to re-emit its interrupt. The falcons "
"do that through ``INTR_RETRIGGER`` (see \"Retriggering a falcon\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:246
msgid "Delivery over PCI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:248
msgid ""
"GIN delivers the tree's interrupts to the CPU as MSI or MSI-X, whichever "
"Linux grants. nova-core requests MSI-X first and falls back to MSI, and "
"never uses INTx."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:252
msgid ""
"MSI has a single message, and every subtree raises that one message, so one "
"Linux IRQ serves the whole tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:255
msgid ""
"MSI-X gives each subtree its own table entry, at the index equal to the "
"subtree number. Linux masks every entry until a driver requests its Linux "
"IRQ number, and a masked entry sends no message: the GPU records the "
"interrupt in the MSI-X pending bit array, where it stays until Linux unmasks "
"the entry. An entry that the driver never requests is never unmasked. A "
"driver that enables a subtree without requesting that subtree's entry loses "
"every interrupt from that subtree, with nothing reported: the leaf and TOP "
"registers show the vector pending and enabled while no handler runs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:265
msgid "The serviced-subtree invariant"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:267
msgid ""
"Every subtree enabled at ``TOP`` has an allocated PCI vector with a "
"registered handler."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:270
msgid ""
"MSI satisfies this with its single message. MSI-X needs one allocated entry "
"per serviced subtree, and a PCI allocation cannot be sparse, so nova-core "
"requests entries 0 through the highest serviced subtree::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:285
msgid ""
"An allocated entry whose subtree nova-core does not service costs nothing. "
"The entry stays masked, and a disabled subtree raises no interrupt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:288
msgid ""
"nova-core services one subtree. The GSP event, vector 155, is in leaf 4, "
"which is in subtree 2. OpenRM's headers place its UVM_SHARED interrupt "
"category in subtree 2 on every chipset that nova-core supports. The self-"
"test doorbell, vector 129, is in the same leaf, and the test allocates its "
"own vectors for it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:294
msgid "Rearming PCI interrupt delivery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu/nova/core/interrupts.rst:296
msgid ""
"A message-signaled interrupt is delivered once per edge, and the PCI side "
"delivers no further interrupt until the CPU rearms it. The rearm operation "
"depends on the GPU family and on the interrupt type that Linux granted:"
msgstr ""

